Q: Flatten a multidimensional array and representing level changes in the result A: <p><strong>Try:</strong></p>

<pre><code>class array_class
{

  public $data = array();
  public $new_array = array();
  public $value = 1; 
  public $number = "";

  public function __construct($data)
  {
    $this-&gt;data = $data;
    $this-&gt;flatten_array($this-&gt;data);
  }

  private function flatten_array($array)
  {
    foreach($array as $key =&gt; $value)
    {            
      if(is_array($value))
      {
        $this-&gt;number = $key;
        $this-&gt;new_array[$this-&gt;value++] = "Start" . $key;
        $this-&gt;flatten_array($value);
      }
      else
      {        
        $this-&gt;new_array[$key] = $value;
        $this-&gt;value++;
      }      
    }         
      $this-&gt;new_array[$this-&gt;value] = "End" . $this-&gt;number;        
  }
}
</code></pre>

<p><strong>Output:</strong></p>

<blockquote>
<pre><code>Array
(
   [one] =&gt; one
   [2] =&gt; Starttwo
   [three] =&gt; three
   [four] =&gt; four
   [5] =&gt; Endtwo
   [five] =&gt; five
   [6] =&gt; Startsix
   [seven] =&gt; seven
   [8] =&gt; Starteight
   [nine] =&gt; nine
   [ten] =&gt; ten
   [11] =&gt; Endeight
)
</code></pre>
</blockquote>

Q: How do I get values from a DataGrid Column? A: <p>To solve this problem cleanly, we need to step back and look at the architecture. Your current approach suffers from a mismatch between Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) principles, data layers, and the MVVM pattern.</p>
<p>Here is a breakdown of why this happens and how to implement a proper, type-safe MVVM solution:</p>
<h3>Architectural Breakdown</h3>
<ol>
<li><p><strong>MVVM is an application-level architectural pattern.</strong> Object-Oriented Programming (OOP), by its very nature, does not mix well with a fragmented, piecemeal implementation. Whenever you try to &quot;stitch&quot; clean OOP principles together with ad-hoc legacy workarounds, severe friction inevitably occurs at those boundaries. When dealing with full-scale application architectures like MVVM, these integration issues are only amplified.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>OOP operates with rich objects, not untyped rows and cells.</strong> Instead of processing raw data structures (like tables or dynamic arrays), true OOP relies on objects that encapsulate state, behavior, methods, and strongly-typed properties.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Your current approach mixes architectural layers.</strong> Based on your code, you are likely executing SQL queries that return standard <code>DataSet</code> or <code>DataTable</code> structures. However, this is not the high-level <strong>Model</strong> in MVVM. This represents the lowest layer — the <em>Data Access Layer (DAL)</em> or the Repository level where the application interacts directly with the database. This infrastructure layer must be encapsulated deep within the Model. The Model should only expose strongly-typed <strong>Entities</strong> or <strong>Data Transfer Objects (DTOs)</strong> to its consumers (in this case, the <code>ViewModel</code>), cleanly reflecting the business state of your application.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>The ViewModel then exposes these Entities via a collection.</strong> Typically, your <code>ViewModel</code> will populate an <code>ObservableCollection&lt;YourDtoClass&gt;</code> and expose it as a property. The View then binds to this property via <code>DataGrid.ItemsSource</code>. By implementing this proper data flow, you completely eliminate the need for reflection tools like <code>TypeDescriptor</code> or <code>PropertyDescriptor</code>. Instead, you work directly with true, strongly-typed <strong>OBJECTS</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>In your View, you are relying on a standard Click event handler.</strong> However, the actions your button needs to trigger are inherently data-driven operations. Code-behind event handlers (<code>Click=&quot;GetDetails&quot;</code>) should strictly be reserved for View-only behavior (like UI animations), and even then, their use is strongly discouraged by the WPF community in favor of cleaner approaches. In a correct MVVM setup, your <code>ViewModel</code> must expose a <strong>Command property</strong> (<code>ICommand</code>), to which the button binds via its <code>Button.Command</code> property.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>To give the command the necessary context, you can pass a parameter to it.</strong> In your specific case, this parameter should be the concrete item from the collection currently being displayed by the <code>DataGrid</code> row. Passing this data from the View directly into the command logic is exactly what the <code>Button.CommandParameter</code> property is designed for.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<h3>Clean Code Example</h3>
<h3>1. Define your Data Object (The Model/DTO)Instead of dealing with generic rows, define a simple class representing your data item:</h3>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public class NestItem
{
    public string NestName { get; set; }
    public DateTime DueDate { get; set; }
    public string OrderStatus { get; set; }
}
</code></pre>
<h4>2. Implement the ViewModelExpose your collection and an <code>ICommand</code>. The command will automatically receive the <code>NestItem</code> object belonging to the row where the button was clicked.</h4>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>using System;
using System.Collections.ObjectModel;
using System.Windows.Input;

public class MainViewModel
{
    // The DataGrid will bind to this strongly-typed collection
    public ObservableCollection&lt;NestItem&gt; NestCollection { get;} = new();

    public ICommand GetDetailsCommand { get; }

    public MainViewModel()
    {
        // Using a standard RelayCommand implementation
        GetDetailsCommand = new RelayCommand&lt;NestItem&gt;(ExecuteGetDetails);
    }

    private void ExecuteGetDetails(NestItem selectedItem)
    {
        if (selectedItem == null) return;

        // Clean, type-safe access without any reflection!
        string name = selectedItem.NestName;

        // Pass the strongly-typed value to your Database/Repository Layer
        LoadAdditionalDetailsFromSql(name);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<h4>3. Update the XAML (The View)Inside the <code>DataGridTemplateColumn</code>, the default <code>DataContext</code> is an individual <code>NestItem</code>.</h4>
<p>We use <code>RelativeSource</code> to route the <code>Command</code> back to the main <code>ViewModel</code> attached to the <code>DataGrid</code>, and we use <code>{Binding}</code> on the <code>CommandParameter</code> to pass the entire current row object directly to the command:</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;DataGrid ItemsSource=&quot;{Binding NestCollection}&quot;
          AutoGenerateColumns=&quot;False&quot;
          ColumnWidth=&quot;*&quot;&gt;
    &lt;DataGrid.Columns&gt;
        &lt;!-- Your other columns here --&gt;

        &lt;DataGridTemplateColumn Header=&quot;Actions&quot;&gt;
            &lt;DataGridTemplateColumn.CellTemplate&gt;
                &lt;DataTemplate&gt;
                    &lt;!-- Redirect command context to the parent DataGrid, pass the row model as a parameter --&gt;
                    &lt;Button Content=&quot;Details&quot;
                            Command=&quot;{Binding DataContext.GetDetailsCommand, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType=DataGrid}}&quot;
                            CommandParameter=&quot;{Binding}&quot; /&gt;
                &lt;/DataTemplate&gt;
            &lt;/DataGridTemplateColumn.CellTemplate&gt;
        &lt;/DataGridTemplateColumn&gt;
    &lt;/DataGrid.Columns&gt;
&lt;/DataGrid&gt;
</code></pre>
<h3>Summary of Changes:</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Old Way:</strong> Button Click -&gt; Code-Behind -&gt; Extract Generic DataContext -&gt; Look up properties using string keys via Reflection.</li>
<li><strong>MVVM Way:</strong> Button Click -&gt; Execute Command -&gt; Directly receive a strongly-typed <code>NestItem</code> object containing all required properties.</li>
</ul>
